  :adr/body
  "Owner instruction 2026-09-10: publish the WAI image capability on
murakumo.cloud as the free `awai-network/hokusai`.

## Published

    POST https://api.murakumo.cloud/v1/images/generations
    {\"model\": \"awai-network/hokusai\", \"prompt\": \"...\", \"size\": \"768x768\"}

Measured live after deploy: 10.5 s, a 1.19 MB PNG. hokusai is a NAME this
fleet owns, bound to `waiREALMIX_v11`; the handler bounds the request and
rewrites `model` to the checkpoint the gateway's bridge already resolves.
Everything downstream was untouched and already worked.

It ships in the JS entry rather than `worker.cljs` because that is the half
that can ship: the compiled worker needs a governed CLJS artifact refresh, and
41 of the 51 siblings its manifest pins have moved. Same seam
`dead_hosted_model.js` uses, for the same reason.

## The name stays closed on text routes, and the test was right

An earlier draft removed hokusai from the reserved set entirely. The existing
test `reserved research models never use the generic relay` caught it: an
image model falling through `/v1/chat/completions` reaches the generic relay,
which is exactly what that boundary exists to prevent. So hokusai is still
refused there -- what changed is the reason. `503 research_model_not_ready`
told a caller to wait for something that had shipped; it is now
`400 wrong_endpoint_for_image_model` naming the route.

That refusal then repeated its own code as its message, because the error
helper took `(status, code)` and used the code for both. A 400 whose whole
purpose is to point somewhere pointed nowhere. Fixed with a default so no
existing caller changed.

## It is NOT served from the fleet's own image nodes, and this is why

benjamin, simeon and dan were converted to WAI-only image nodes the same day
and render this checkpoint in 72 s at 768x768. They do not serve this route.

**The pull queue cannot carry an image, by design.** `bounded-queue-output`
in worker.cljs says a remote worker's output \"must not turn the run ledger
into an unbounded blob store\" and caps result text at 16,384 characters; a
768x768 PNG is ~1.2 MB of base64. That is a deliberate constraint, not an
oversight, and working around it would be defeating a decision rather than
extending one.

Carrying bytes therefore needs somewhere to put them -- kotobase, per the
workspace persistence policy -- and a write credential on the node. Measured:
`PUT https://kotobase.net/ipfs/<cid>` from benjamin answers 401, and
`join.env` holds only `MURAKUMO_SERVICE_TOKEN`. Minting a presigned upload
instead lands back in worker.cljs, which is the blocked half.

The node-side work is landed and tested against that destination anyway:
`murakumo.infer.image-job` (validation, the ComfyUI graph, history parsing)
and the worker's render branch, which dispatches on the `:kind` the queue has
always carried and nothing read. What is missing is one thing, named here so
nobody re-derives it: **a bytes path from a node to a caller.**

## Why the queue and not a tunnel, when the tunnel would have worked

gad is fronted by a Cloudflare Tunnel to a bridge on :8189, and copying that
shape for the minis was the obvious move. It was rejected on the same day's
measurements: **the minis have no reliable inbound path.** issachar and joseph
were up, on the LAN, with port 22 open and no credential or route that reached
them, because their only way in was an overlay that had dropped. An image tier
fronted by an inbound tunnel inherits exactly that failure. The pull queue is
outbound-only and a node behind a broken tunnel simply stops claiming.

So the destination is right and the transport is missing, rather than the
transport being easy and the destination being fragile.

## Verified after deploy

    hokusai on /v1/images/generations      200, 1.19 MB PNG, 10.5 s
    hokusai on /v1/chat/completions        400 wrong_endpoint_for_image_model
    giemon on /v1/chat/completions         401, unchanged
    no prompt / n=4 / size 4096x4096       400 with a code each
    animagine-xl-4.0 on the same route     200, unaffected

## Follow-up

- The bytes path above. Until it exists, hokusai renders on gad.
- A source-level guard in `dead_hosted_model.test.mjs` pinned the exact
  argument to `legacyWorker.fetch`, so wiring anything else through the same
  call read as breakage. It now pins the shape of the public fall-through.
  Generalising it to every `legacyWorker.fetch` was tried and was wrong:
  `edgeCompletion`'s internal enqueue and result calls correctly skip that
  rewrite, and the broad version failed on them."}]
